{
  "slug": "design-hit-counter",
  "title": "Hit Counter Window Queries",
  "topic": "binary-search",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "pattern": "binary search / counting in a trailing window over sorted events",
  "statement": "A service logs the arrival time of every request in the array `times`, where each entry is an integer timestamp. Timestamps may repeat and are not given in any particular order. A query at time `q` asks how many logged requests arrived inside the window of length `window` that ends at `q`, that is, requests whose timestamp `t` satisfies `q - window < t <= q`.\n\nGiven `window`, `times` and the array of query times `queries`, return an array whose i-th entry is the number of logged requests inside the window ending at `queries[i]`. Queries are answered in the order they are given, and the same logged request is counted by every query whose window contains it.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= window <= 1000`, `1 <= times.length <= 200`, `0 <= times[i] <= 10000`, `1 <= queries.length <= 200` and `0 <= queries[i] <= 10000`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "window = 1, times = [0], queries = [0]",
      "output": "[1]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "window = 952, times = [152, 7508, 9224], queries = [1200, 8808, 3492, 8288]",
      "output": "[0, 0, 0, 1]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(window: number, times: number[], queries: number[])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "The answer for a query does not care which requests are in the window, only how many, and that count is the difference of two prefix counts: those at most q and those at most q - window.",
    "Prefix counts over a sorted array are indices, so sort the timestamps once and look up each prefix count with a halving search instead of a scan.",
    "Write your search as 'first index whose value is at least x'. The window condition q - window < t <= q becomes two such searches, at x = q - window + 1 and x = q + 1."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force answers each query independently with a linear scan over `times`, counting every timestamp that satisfies t <= q and t > q - window. That is one full pass per query. The optimal makes a single observation: the count only depends on how many timestamps are at most q and how many are at most q - window, so sorting `times` once turns every query into two rank lookups. Binary search for the first index whose value is >= q + 1 gives the number of timestamps at most q, and the first index whose value is >= q - window + 1 gives the number at most q - window; their difference is the answer. Lower-bound search is written over half-open index ranges so the result can be 0 or times.length without a special case.",
    "why_optimal": "Sorting takes O(times.length log times.length), which is unavoidable for the rank lookups, and each query then costs two searches of O(log times.length), so the total is dominated by the sort whenever the two arrays are comparable in size. The brute force is correct but repeats the same comparison work per query, which is exactly the factor of queries.length. At the given caps both finish far inside a millisecond; the asymptotics matter only when the log grows by orders of magnitude. A single sorted sweep with a moving left pointer is an equally good alternative for the optimal, O(times.length log times.length + queries.length log queries.length).",
    "pitfalls": "The window is half-open on the left: a request exactly `window` units before the query is outside, so the lower bound must be q - window + 1 and not q - window. Because `window` can be large relative to the timestamps, q - window + 1 can be negative, which is fine for a lower bound but breaks any solution that indexes buckets directly by timestamp. Duplicate timestamps must each be counted, so the two ranks have to be taken over the full sorted array and never de-duplicated. Sorting must not mutate the caller's array if the same input array is reused by the harness, and in JavaScript the default `sort()` compares as strings, so `sort((a, b) => a - b)` is required."
  },
